# Free, auto-refreshing list of US-listed companies

This sets up a CSV of US-exchange-listed companies (tickers, industry,
exchange, state of incorporation, IPO date where available, etc.) that
refreshes itself on a schedule -- entirely on free tiers, with the compute
running in the cloud so your computer doesn't need to be on.

**Files in this folder:**
- `fetch_us_stock_universe.py` -- the script that builds the CSV
- `.github/workflows/refresh-stock-list.yml` -- tells GitHub to run the
  script automatically on a schedule
- `README.md` -- this file

**Data sources (all free, no credit card):**
- Nasdaq Trader symbol directory -- base list of every ticker + an ETF flag
  used to exclude funds
- SEC EDGAR -- industry (SIC code), state of incorporation, fiscal year end,
  former names
- Alpha Vantage (optional) -- IPO/first-listed date

---

## Step 1: Create a free GitHub account and repository

1. Go to [github.com](https://github.com) and sign up (free).
2. Click the **+** in the top right -> **New repository**.
3. Name it something like `us-stock-universe`. Public is simplest (and
   gives you unlimited free Actions minutes); Private also works but has a
   monthly minutes cap on the free plan -- this job easily fits within it
   for a weekly schedule.
4. Click **Create repository**.

## Step 2: Add the files to your repo

Easiest way -- no installs required:

1. In your new repo, click **Add file -> Create new file**.
2. Name it `fetch_us_stock_universe.py`, paste in the contents of that file
   from this folder, and commit it.
3. Click **Add file -> Create new file** again. This time type the name
   exactly as `.github/workflows/refresh-stock-list.yml` (typing the slashes
   automatically creates the folders). Paste in that file's contents and
   commit.

(If you're comfortable with git locally, you can instead clone the repo,
copy both files in, and `git push` -- same result.)

## Step 3 (optional): Get a free Alpha Vantage API key

This only adds the "IPO/First Listed Date" column -- skip it if you don't
need that field.

1. Go to https://www.alphavantage.co/support/#api-key
2. Enter an email, get a free key instantly (no payment info required).

## Step 4: Add your SEC User-Agent (and API key) as repo secrets

The SEC requires every request to identify a real contact -- this isn't
optional on their end, so set it here rather than editing the script.

1. In your repo, go to **Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions**.
2. Click **New repository secret**.
   - Name: `SEC_USER_AGENT`
   - Value: something like `Jane Doe jane@example.com` (your real name/org
     and a real email -- SEC blocks generic or blank User-Agents)
3. Click **New repository secret** again (only if you did Step 3):
   - Name: `ALPHA_VANTAGE_API_KEY`
   - Value: the key you got from Alpha Vantage

## Step 5: Run it and check the result

1. Go to the **Actions** tab in your repo.
2. Click **Refresh US company list** on the left, then **Run workflow**
   (the `workflow_dispatch` trigger added a manual button for this).
3. It'll take roughly 30-60 minutes for a full run (the SEC lookup is one
   request per company, deliberately rate-limited so it doesn't hammer
   their servers). Watch progress under the running job's logs.
4. When it finishes, `us_listed_companies.csv` will appear in your repo,
   and the workflow will keep re-running automatically every Monday
   (edit the `cron` line in the workflow file to change the schedule --
   e.g. `0 13 * * *` for daily).

**To test quickly first** instead of waiting an hour: in the workflow file,
add `MAX_COMPANIES: "50"` under the `env:` section of the "Run fetch
script" step. Remove it once you've confirmed everything works.

## Step 6: Connect Excel so it refreshes automatically

Once the CSV exists in your repo, grab its "raw" URL: open the file on
github.com and click **Raw** -- copy that URL
(looks like `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<you>/<repo>/main/us_listed_companies.csv`).

In Excel:
1. **Data** tab -> **Get Data** -> **From File** -> **From Text/CSV**... or,
   on some versions, **Get Data** -> **From Other Sources** -> **From Web**,
   and paste that raw URL.
2. Click **Load**.
3. Right-click the query in **Queries & Connections** -> **Properties**.
4. Check **Refresh data when opening the file**, and optionally
   **Refresh every N minutes** while the workbook is open.

Now every time you open the workbook, Excel pulls the latest version of
the file straight from GitHub.

**Bonus option -- Google Sheets:** paste
`=IMPORTDATA("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<you>/<repo>/main/us_listed_companies.csv")`
into cell A1 of a Google Sheet. Google Sheets refreshes `IMPORTDATA`
periodically on its own, even while the sheet is closed -- fully free, no
setup beyond that one formula.

---

## What's in the CSV

| Column | Source | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Ticker | Nasdaq Trader | |
| Company Name | SEC (falls back to Nasdaq's name) | |
| Exchange | Nasdaq Trader | NASDAQ, NYSE, NYSE American, NYSE Arca, Cboe BZX |
| Industry (SIC Description) | SEC | SEC's standard industry classification |
| SIC Code | SEC | numeric code behind the description |
| CIK | SEC | SEC's unique company ID, handy for further lookups |
| State of Incorporation | SEC | |
| Fiscal Year End | SEC | MMDD format |
| IPO/First Listed Date | Alpha Vantage (optional) | blank if no key configured or data unavailable |
| Former Name(s) | SEC | semicolon-separated |
| SEC Exchange(s) | SEC | SEC's own record of listing exchange(s) |

## Known limitations (worth knowing about)

- **"Actual company" filtering is name/flag-based.** It excludes anything
  flagged as an ETF by Nasdaq, plus names containing words like "fund,"
  "trust," "warrant," "preferred," "unit," "SPAC," etc. This is very
  effective but not perfect -- a handful of edge cases may slip through in
  either direction. Adjust `EXCLUDE_NAME_KEYWORDS` in the script to tune it.
- **Not every ticker gets full SEC enrichment.** Share-class and
  special-security tickers are sometimes formatted differently between
  Nasdaq's directory and SEC's ticker map (e.g. `BRK.A` vs `BRK-A`), so a
  small percentage won't match up to a CIK and will only have the basic
  Nasdaq fields. Common single-class stocks (the vast majority) match fine.
- **IPO date isn't a field SEC tracks directly.** Alpha Vantage's
  `ipoDate` is the best free source for it, but it isn't complete or
  guaranteed accurate for older listings.
- **Runtime.** The SEC lookup step is intentionally slow (about 9
  requests/second) to respect SEC's fair-access limits, so a full run
  covering ~5,000-6,000 companies takes roughly 30-60 minutes. This runs
  in GitHub's cloud, not on your computer, so it doesn't block you from
  doing anything else.
